EXECUTIVE FINDING

This case is far more interesting than:

“780,000 people were breached.”

The real security question is:

What happens when the company selling the firewall may itself become the path around the firewall?

Marquis Software Solutions suffered a ransomware attack on August 14, 2025.

Marquis says the attacker gained unauthorized access to its network and accessed client data containing personally identifiable information belonging to customers of financial institutions that use Marquis.

Months later, Marquis concluded that the intrusion was connected to a separate security incident involving SonicWall's MySonicWall cloud-backup environment.

That SonicWall incident is independently confirmed.

SonicWall initially said backup configuration files associated with fewer than 5% of its firewall install base had been accessed.

After investigation with Mandiant, SonicWall revised that assessment dramatically:

firewall configuration backup files belonging to all customers who had used the affected cloud-backup service had been accessed.

Those configuration files contained encrypted credentials and network configuration information capable of increasing the risk of targeted attacks.

What remains disputed is the most consequential part:

Did material stolen from SonicWall actually enable the August 14 compromise of Marquis?

Marquis says yes.

SonicWall has publicly said it has not seen evidence establishing that connection.

That means this case belongs in SecurityReceipts, not merely BreachLedger.

THE CENTRAL QUESTION

Traditional vendor security looks like:

COMPANY
   ↓
FIREWALL
   ↓
INTERNET

But this incident raises another architecture:

COMPANY
   ↓
FIREWALL
   ↓
CLOUD MANAGEMENT / BACKUP PLATFORM
   ↓
SECURITY VENDOR

If that management plane is compromised:

SECURITY VENDOR BREACH
        ↓
FIREWALL CONFIGURATION DATA
        ↓
CREDENTIAL / SECRET EXPOSURE
        ↓
KNOWLEDGE OF CUSTOMER PERIMETER
        ↓
TARGETED ATTACK
        ↓
CUSTOMER NETWORK

That is the nightmare scenario.

A control intended to reduce risk can become a concentration point for risk.
